      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "| When | What happened | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10-24 00:59 | Core first refuses to load `decebal-dobrica-at-gmail-com`: needs 2,943,809,744 B, has 1,881,874,432 B free | Core log |\n| 01:15, 14:00, 15:40, 17:10 | Core restarts four times. From about 13:00 the tenant is refused continuously, 142–231 times an hour | orange markers; Core log |\n| 15:40 | Control plane v120 starts with the egress guard; 429s begin | red marker; edge status counts |\n| 20:17 | Core also refuses the Longhand control plane's tenant (needs 2,475,612,384 B): 625 refusals in the next hour | Core log |\n| 21:51 | Core resized by hand from 8 GB to 16 GB; the machine restarts | memory size steps 7,942 → 15,993 MiB |\n| 21:55 | Core back with 9.4 GB free at 0.3% CPU, answering every egress reservation with 503 in 0 ms. Answered responses fall from ~8,400 to 0–16 per 15 min | Core log; edge status counts |\n| 22:25 | `ControlPlaneServingFailures` (added afterwards) would have fired | rule replayed |\n| 10-25 10:05 | Core v56 (`fe6be49`) with the write-ahead-log fix starts; 503s stop | red marker; `fly releases` |\n| 10:05–11:15 | 949 control-plane 500s, peaking at 256 per 5 min, then gone. Source not identified | `fly_app_http_responses_count` |"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 302,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Why every read failed, and why nobody noticed",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 14,
        "y": 45,
        "w": 10,
        "h": 14
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Cause.** The control plane reserves egress bytes in Core's ledger before each read and completes the reservation after. The ledger lives in Core's system store, which is write-ahead-log only and never checkpointed. On every boot its writer reopened the *oldest* segment, so entries appended after a restart replayed near the start of history, out of order. An out-of-order Reserve or Complete invalidates the tenant's account, and every later Reserve answers 503. `fe6be49` (#351) recovers in sequence order.\n\n**Unnoticed.** The only alerts watched for *too many* bytes; refusals lower bytes, so the outage looked like the bandwidth problem healing. Core's 503 log line names no reason. No AllSource metric was scraped.\n\n**Still open.** The system store is still never checkpointed. Core still refuses a whole tenant that doesn't fit in memory. The control plane runs `300f852`. The post-recovery 500s are unattributed."
      }
    },
